===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[group]] of [[individual#Noun|individuals]] or [[company|companies]] formed to [[transact]] some specific [[business]], or to [[promote]] a common [[interest#Noun|interest]]; a self-[[coordinate#Verb|coordinating]] group.
#: {{ux|en|a gambling '''syndicate'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Julian Thomas (journalist)|Julian Thomas]]|chapter=Maafu, Prince of Tonga|title=Cannibals & Convicts: Notes of Personal Experiences in the Western Pacific|location=London; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Cassell (publisher)|Cassel & Company, Limited]]|year=1886|page=24|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FlMWAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA24|oclc=780482546|passage=The worst deed ever alleged against the early sandalwood traders was that a number of natives were suffocated in a cave at Vaté, in the New Hebrides, by the smoke from a fire built at its mouth. But this was done by order of [[w:Enele Maʻafu|Maafu]], who, on behalf of an Australian '''syndicate''' of highly-respectable merchants and church-members, was "bossing" an expedition in search of sandalwood through the New Hebrides.}}
## {{lb|en|crime}} A group of [[gangster]]s engaged in [[organized crime]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=John Madinger|chapter=Case Development|title=Confidential Informant: Law Enforcement’s Most Valuable Tool|location=Boca Raton, Fla.|publisher=w:CRC Press|year=1999|page=178|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=aK1fo5LhgOYC&pg=PA178|isbn=978-0-8493-0709-6|passage=Held at the estate of Mafia boss [[w:Joseph Barbara (mobster)|Joseph Barbara]] in Apalachin, NY, the meeting called by [[w:Vito Genovese|[Vito] Genovese]] in November 1957 brought over 100 mobsters from around the country to cement his power over the national crime '''syndicate'''.}}
## {{lb|en|mass media}} A group of [[media]] companies, or an [[agency]], formed to [[acquire]] [[content#Noun|content]] such as [[article]]s, [[cartoon]]s, etc., and to [[publish]] it in [[multiple]] [[outlet]]s; a [[chain#Noun|chain]] of newspapers or other media outlets [[manage]]d by such an [[organization]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Wiliam L. Andrews|chapter=The Apprentice Years: Experiments and Decisions|title=The Literary Career of Charles W. Chesnutt|location=Baton Rouge, La.; London|publisher=w:Louisiana State University Press|year=1980|page=17|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=D35joFzIMZ8C&pg=PA17|isbn=978-0-8071-0673-0|passage=In the mid-1880s, [[w:Charles W. Chesnutt|Charles W[addell] Chesnutt]] broke into print inauspiciously as one member of the stable of writers who supplied humorous sketches and mildly sentimental or didactic squibs for [[w:S. S. McClure|S[amuel] S[idney] McClure]]'s newspaper '''syndicate'''.}}
# The [[office]] or [[jurisdiction]] of a [[syndic]]; a [[body]] or [[council]] of syndics.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Gilbert Burnet|G[ilbert] Burnet]]|title=Some Letters Containing, an Account of What Seemed Most Remarkable in Switzerland, Italy, &c. Written by G. Burnett, D.D. to T[he] H[onourable] [[w:Robert Boyle|R[obert] B[oyle]]]|location=Rotterdam|publisher=Printed by Abraham Acher, bookseller by the [[w:Royal Exchange, London|Exchange]]|year=1686|pages=10–11|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=NDO5l-igtmsC&pg=PA11|oclc=702552544|passage=The ſoveraintie lies in the Council of 200, and this Council chuſes out of its number 25, who are the leſſer Council; {{...}} they are choſen by a ſort of ballet, ſo that it is not known for whom they give their votes, which is an effectual method to ſuppreſs factions and reſentments; ſince in a competition no man can know who voted for him or againſt him: yet the election is not ſo carried, but that the whole Town is in an intrigue concerning it: for ſince the being of the little Council leads one to the '''Sindicat''', which is the chieffe honor of the State: this dignitie is courted here, with as active and ſolicitous an ambition as appears elſewhere for greater matters.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=&#91;{{w|India Office}}, {{w|Government of the United Kingdom}}]|title=Copy of Correspondence with the Indian Government, Showing the Progress of the Measures Adopted for Carrying Out the Education Despatch of the 19th Day of July 1854. [Bye-laws for the Government of the University of Madras.]|journal=Accounts and Papers: Twenty-one Volumes. [...] Session 2, 31 May&nbsp;– 13 August 1859|location=[London|publisher=[[w:Office of Public Sector Information|Her Majesty's Stationery Office]]]|year=1859|volume=10 (East India (Education)), part II (Madras); volume XXIV, part II|page=367|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LdISAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA367|oclc=380264703|passage=The executive government of the [[w:University of Madras|University [of Madras]]] shall be vested in a '''syndicate''', consisting of the Vice-Chancellor and six of the fellows, who shall be elected for one year by the several faculties, at the annual meetng of the senate, {{...}}}}

